WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.950
Make the database subnet of a VPC private, so that only the app tier can reach it.

00:00:05.950 --> 00:00:16.050
The VPC demo-3tier has a web, an app, and a database subnet, with one virtual machine in each. Right now, every subnet reaches every other one.

00:00:16.050 --> 00:00:20.640
Open the action menu of the demo-db subnet,

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:23.450
and select Edit Config.

00:00:23.450 --> 00:00:30.070
Under Private Subnet, select Enabled. A private subnet drops all traffic that comes from outside the subnet.

00:00:30.070 --> 00:00:33.690
Add an exception for the subnets that may still connect.

00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:41.510
Enter the app subnet, 10.230.2.0/24. The web subnet is not on the list.

00:00:41.510 --> 00:00:49.330
Select Save. Kube-OVN updates the subnet's rules at once, without restarting any virtual machine.

00:00:49.330 --> 00:00:53.340
Go to Virtual Machines and open the console of demo-web.

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:02.220
Log in as sles, with the password that cloud-init set.

00:01:04.820 --> 00:01:12.130
Request the database tier's port 5432.

00:01:12.130 --> 00:01:17.660
The connection times out. The web subnet is not allowed into the private subnet.

00:01:17.660 --> 00:01:20.720
Now open the console of demo-app.

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:25.460
Log in the same way.

00:01:28.130 --> 00:01:31.880
Send the same request.

00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:40.560
The answer arrives. The app subnet is on the allow list, so the app tier still reaches the database.

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:51.149
The database subnet is private: the app tier still reaches it, the web tier is dropped. Next, you limit traffic down to single ports with access control lists.
